1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,164
A stakeholder's expectations rarely come from your golden set.

2
00:00:04,164 --> 00:00:09,085
They come from a keynote demo, a competitor's launch post, or a headline about a

3
00:00:09,085 --> 00:00:13,822
frontier model, none of which mention the failure category your own eval work

4
00:00:13,822 --> 00:00:14,683
already found.

5
00:00:14,683 --> 00:00:17,267
Your job isn't to dampen their enthusiasm.

6
00:00:17,267 --> 00:00:22,004
It's to replace an expectation built on marketing with one built on evidence.

7
00:00:22,004 --> 00:00:27,171
That's a different skill from simply being the person who says "it's not ready yet."

8
00:00:27,171 --> 00:00:32,338
Vague caution earns exactly as little trust as vague hype, because neither one gives

9
00:00:32,338 --> 00:00:36,952
a stakeholder anything they can check later against what actually happened.

10
00:00:37,502 --> 00:00:42,385
This gap isn't a communication failure on the stakeholder's part, and treating it

11
00:00:42,385 --> 00:00:46,606
that way is the fastest way to lose the conversation before it starts.

12
00:00:46,606 --> 00:00:51,429
Where would their expectation have come from, if not the same marketing everyone

13
00:00:51,429 --> 00:00:52,032
else sees?

14
00:00:52,032 --> 00:00:56,674
Assume good faith about that, and spend the conversation closing the gap, not

15
00:00:56,674 --> 00:00:59,266
arguing they were wrong to have one at all.

16
00:00:59,266 --> 00:01:04,150
Think of the stakeholder relationship the same way 9.5 asked you to think about a

17
00:01:04,150 --> 00:01:08,792
leadership dashboard: something that has to survive contact with a bad number

18
00:01:08,792 --> 00:01:12,952
eventually, not something optimised to look good in this one meeting.

19
00:01:12,952 --> 00:01:17,655
A relationship that's only ever heard good news breaks the first time it hears

20
00:01:17,655 --> 00:01:18,499
anything else.

21
00:01:19,049 --> 00:01:24,036
Read the right column as a direct export of tools you've already built, not new

22
00:01:24,036 --> 00:01:26,183
language to invent under pressure.

23
00:01:26,183 --> 00:01:31,360
A pass rate from 4.5, a scorecard decision from 7.4, a blast radius sort from 6.6,

24
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:35,905
each one already exists before the stakeholder conversation even starts.

25
00:01:35,905 --> 00:01:41,146
Notice what every right-column sentence has that the left column lacks: something a

26
00:01:41,146 --> 00:01:43,040
stakeholder could check later.

27
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:48,153
"Ninety-four percent, fails on this category" is a claim reality can prove wrong,

28
00:01:48,153 --> 00:01:51,121
which is exactly what makes it trustworthy now.

29
00:01:51,121 --> 00:01:56,172
"It's pretty much ready" can't be checked against anything, so it never earns or

30
00:01:56,172 --> 00:01:57,308
loses credibility.

31
00:01:57,308 --> 00:02:00,212
It just gets forgotten until something breaks.

32
00:02:00,212 --> 00:02:05,515
Notice, too, that the right column is shorter to say than the left, not longer, once

33
00:02:05,515 --> 00:02:07,978
you've actually got the number in hand.

34
00:02:07,978 --> 00:02:13,091
"Ninety-four percent, fails on this category" takes about as long to say as "it's

35
00:02:13,091 --> 00:02:17,195
pretty much ready." The specificity isn't extra work in the room.

36
00:02:17,195 --> 00:02:22,057
It's extra work beforehand, done once, back in Section four, long before this

37
00:02:22,057 --> 00:02:23,698
conversation ever happens.

38
00:02:24,248 --> 00:02:29,440
This is also the direct payoff of everything this course has built toward, stated as

39
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:34,262
plainly as possible: can you produce these three sentences on request, for the

40
00:02:34,262 --> 00:02:35,869
feature you own right now?

41
00:02:35,869 --> 00:02:38,712
That's the actual test, not the roadmap slide.

42
00:02:39,262 --> 00:02:41,843
Notice the good column doesn't demo worse.

43
00:02:41,843 --> 00:02:47,005
It demos honestly, using a boundary case straight out of 4.2's golden set instead of

44
00:02:47,005 --> 00:02:47,804
avoiding one.

45
00:02:47,804 --> 00:02:50,693
The difference shows up later, not in the room.

46
00:02:50,693 --> 00:02:55,855
A stakeholder who's never seen the feature fail treats the first failure as a broken

47
00:02:55,855 --> 00:02:56,347
promise.

48
00:02:56,347 --> 00:03:01,386
A stakeholder who's already seen a named limitation, in a controlled demo, on your

49
00:03:01,386 --> 00:03:04,827
terms, treats the same failure as an expected edge case.

50
00:03:04,827 --> 00:03:09,928
Identical failure, completely different trust outcome, decided entirely by what was

51
00:03:09,928 --> 00:03:11,157
shown weeks earlier.

52
00:03:11,157 --> 00:03:15,889
Choosing the boundary case deliberately is harder than it sounds, because the

53
00:03:15,889 --> 00:03:20,929
instinct under pressure, especially before an important stakeholder meeting, pulls

54
00:03:20,929 --> 00:03:23,387
hard toward the safest possible showing.

55
00:03:23,387 --> 00:03:25,108
Resist it specifically here.

56
00:03:25,108 --> 00:03:29,840
The whole value of naming a limitation before someone finds it is lost if the

57
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:33,711
limitation shown is a trivial one nobody would have hit anyway.

58
00:03:33,711 --> 00:03:38,751
Pick the real one, the case that actually worries you, not a token weakness chosen

59
00:03:38,751 --> 00:03:40,349
for how harmless it looks.

60
00:03:40,899 --> 00:03:45,946
Say the honest caveat, because the instinct after being burned once is to swing hard

61
00:03:45,946 --> 00:03:49,491
toward maximum caution, and that instinct has its own cost.

62
00:03:49,491 --> 00:03:54,479
If every estimate you give is quietly padded three times over, a stakeholder learns

63
00:03:54,479 --> 00:03:59,346
to mentally discount everything you say, which means your actual signal, the case

64
00:03:59,346 --> 00:04:02,531
where something really is at risk, stops landing too.

65
00:04:02,531 --> 00:04:04,273
The goal was never pessimism.

66
00:04:04,273 --> 00:04:09,261
It was calibration, the same word 4.8 used for a golden set's own honesty: a number

67
00:04:09,261 --> 00:04:14,008
that's right about as often as it claims to be, not padded in either direction.

68
00:04:14,008 --> 00:04:18,875
Watch for the specific symptom of over-correction: a stakeholder starts routinely

69
00:04:18,875 --> 00:04:23,923
double-checking numbers you've already checked, or quietly building their own buffer

70
00:04:23,923 --> 00:04:25,245
on top of your buffer.

71
00:04:25,245 --> 00:04:27,227
That's not caution on their part.

72
00:04:27,227 --> 00:04:32,155
It's the market price of a track record that's stopped being informative, and it's

73
00:04:32,155 --> 00:04:35,880
exactly as expensive to earn back as trust lost the other way.

74
00:04:35,880 --> 00:04:40,567
Aim for a number that's wrong in both directions roughly as often as an honest

75
00:04:40,567 --> 00:04:45,014
estimate should be, not one that's never once come in worse than promised.

76
00:04:45,014 --> 00:04:49,461
A track record with zero misses in either direction isn't a sign of skill.

77
00:04:49,461 --> 00:04:54,508
It's usually a sign of padding thick enough to hide the real variance underneath it.

78
00:04:55,058 --> 00:04:59,982
That's the standard worth holding the next update to, and the exercise is worth

79
00:04:59,982 --> 00:05:04,594
running on a real sentence you were about to send, not a hypothetical one.

80
00:05:04,594 --> 00:05:09,517
Most hedges, looked at honestly, already have a real number sitting behind them

81
00:05:09,517 --> 00:05:13,444
somewhere, a pass rate, a scorecard result, a blast radius row.

82
00:05:13,444 --> 00:05:16,311
The hedge isn't hiding an absence of evidence.

83
00:05:16,311 --> 00:05:20,237
It's usually hiding evidence nobody bothered to quote directly.

84
00:05:20,237 --> 00:05:25,161
Do this exercise on the update you were about to send today, not a hypothetical

85
00:05:25,161 --> 00:05:25,846
future one.

86
00:05:25,846 --> 00:05:30,895
The habit only forms by catching a real hedge in the moment it's about to go out,

87
00:05:30,895 --> 00:05:35,756
not by reviewing old messages after the fact when there's nothing left to fix.

88
00:05:36,306 --> 00:05:38,058
One thing before you move on.

89
00:05:38,058 --> 00:05:39,326
Find the hedge first.

90
00:05:39,326 --> 00:05:44,401
It's usually the sentence that felt safest to write, which is exactly why it's worth

91
00:05:44,401 --> 00:05:46,696
checking for a real number underneath.

92
00:05:46,696 --> 00:05:51,106
Then look at your next planned demo and add one case you know will show a

93
00:05:51,106 --> 00:05:55,033
limitation, on purpose, before someone else finds it by accident.

94
00:05:55,033 --> 00:05:56,905
Neither step requires new work.

95
00:05:56,905 --> 00:06:01,798
The number behind the hedge and the boundary case in your golden set both already

96
00:06:01,798 --> 00:06:02,161
exist.

97
00:06:02,161 --> 00:06:07,054
This is entirely about what you choose to say out loud, not what you have left to

98
00:06:07,054 --> 00:06:07,416
build.

99
00:06:07,416 --> 00:06:12,430
Next lecture is the AI roadmap that doesn't lie: how the same discipline applies to

100
00:06:12,430 --> 00:06:15,873
a whole quarter's commitments, not just one conversation.
